    id: "4d340f31f723357ea66b",
    cat: "ai",
    categories: ["ai"],
    title: "India flags Chinese-linked AI tools in hacking campaign against financial firms",
    summary: "India's cybersecurity agency said the attackers used publicly available AI tools to automate break-ins \"at machine speed\", calling the threat \"active and ongoing\".",
    preview: "India's cybersecurity agency said the attackers used publicly available AI tools to automate break-ins \"at machine speed\", calling the threat \"active and ongoing\". BENGALURU/MUMBAI: India's cybersecurity agency named the hacking group behind a wave of AI-driven attacks on financial firms and identified its tools, at least two of them linked to China, adding India to a growing list of countries where such tools have been used against banks.",
    body: "India's cybersecurity agency said the attackers used publicly available AI tools to automate break-ins \"at machine speed\", calling the threat \"active and ongoing\".\n\nBENGALURU/MUMBAI: India's cybersecurity agency named the hacking group behind a wave of AI-driven attacks on financial firms and identified its tools, at least two of them linked to China, adding India to a growing list of countries where such tools have been used against banks.\n\nThe group, which the Indian Computer Emergency Response Team (CERT-In) calls \"Crafty Falcon,\" has breached non-bank lenders, fintech firms and cryptocurrency exchanges, according to an advisory issued on Wednesday (Nov 4).\n\nCERT-In did not say who was behind the breaches or where the organisation operates.\n\nThe advisory came after a spate of cyberattacks on Indian banks and non-bank lenders in recent weeks, even as authorities issued warnings and the Reserve Bank of India tightened cybersecurity checks.\n\nCERT-In said the attackers use publicly available AI tools to automate break-ins \"at machine speed\", calling the threat \"active and ongoing\".\n\nIt named four tools: ARTEX, PentAGI, Cairn and CyberStrikeAI. Security researchers have linked at least two of them to China.\n\nChina-made ARTEX has been used in attacks on South Korean banks since late September, cybersecurity firm CrowdStrike has said.\n\nIt said a China-linked group also used the tool in an espionage campaign targeting more than 200 organisations in at least 14 countries.\n\nCERT-In did not name the affected companies or say how much money was stolen.\n\nThe agency did not say the attacks were state-sponsored and the tools it named have been freely available online. ARTEX, however, was made private by its developer last month after it was linked to the South Korean attacks.\n\nCERT-In, the Indian Cybercrime Coordination Centre and India's IT ministry did not immediately respond to requests for comment.\n\nPAYMENT INTERFACES TARGETED\n\nCERT-In said Crafty Falcon \"surveys its targets with machine intelligence\" and moves money by invoking victims' own payment and disbursement systems.\n\nAttacks were timed around holidays, weekends and other low-monitoring periods to delay detection. Firms that had fixed flaws after earlier alerts were attacked again after incomplete remediations were made, it said.\n\nReuters previously reported, citing a police complaint, that last month two Mumbai-based non-bank lenders lost about US$2 million in more than 700 unauthorised instant transfers from accounts of India's third-largest lender ICICI Bank on Oct 8 and 9.\n\nThe complaint said hackers compromised its application programming interfaces (APIs), the software links banks use to connect with non-bank lenders and fintech firms to send loan-disbursal instructions.\n\nState Bank of India, the country's largest lender, disclosed a dark-web leak of 3,897 customer records last month. Spandana Sphoorty Financial, a non-bank lender, reported unauthorised debits from a bank account on Nov 1, and AU Small Finance Bank said it was probing a potential customer-data leak.\n\nCERT-In did not say which incidents were linked to Crafty Falcon.",
